How's this for an interpretation?  Nested bags only provided
protection because they encompass something (nearly) completely.  A
sack has to have its twist tie tied, or its drawstring drawn, or
whatever, in order to provide protection.  Normally, you do close a
bag before you drop it into a bag of holding.  It is still closed when
you take it out to grab its contents.  However, if you were to open
the inner bag up, while it contained a wand of cancellation (e.g.),
the wand would be exposed fully to the bag, which would explode
(possibly taking your hand with it?).

Using this idea, it logically would be perfectly safe to take the bag
out using the current system, but dangerous to use the one turn nested
command.  Also, untying strings or pulling on elastic fasteners could
account for a wide range of delays, if you wanted to put them in.  I
could see anywhere from 1 turn for the whole operation to 1 turn per
bag making sense, depending on the type of fastening device.

